From 9747fa5062189f346b8f1fcb35ccd074cfa45e82 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 09:55:06 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] Stall watchdog: say where the emulation thread is stuck

When the game shows no frame for five seconds and the scheduler is not
idle, the watchdog's own thread said only how long, when the scheduler
last went idle, and the last pad words. That cannot tell a loop in the
game's code from an HLE call the host never returned from; a player's
freeze in Dissidia 012's menus logged nothing more.

The runtime now keeps what a watchdog on another thread can read
(Runtime::activity()): the HLE function in progress, and running counts
of HLE calls and of starvation boundaries. The emulation thread is the
only writer, with relaxed atomics: a few plain stores per HLE call, and
nothing printed unless the game stalls. Runtime::hle_function_name()
turns the function back into library::name.

The watchdog compares those counts a second apart and adds two lines:
the guest thread, the function last dispatched, pc and ra, the HLE call
it is in; then a verdict: every thread waits (the idle dump has the
rest), stuck in the host's handling of one call, looping in the game's
code with no HLE call (it is being preempted), looping through HLE calls,
or neither (a loop within one recompiled function, or host code it
called).

Checked with Dissidia 012 on Windows (llvm-mingw build): a test-only
sleep of 8 s inside sceDisplaySetFrameBuf reported "stuck in the port's
handling of sceDisplay::sceDisplaySetFrameBuf"; a guest branch-to-self
run by the interpreter reported pc inside the loop and "looping in the
game's code". A normal boot printed nothing with the limit at 0.5 s.
Unthrottled title screen: guest time and speed unchanged (0.100 ms,
1869% against 1871%). The 21 framework tests pass (ad hoc excluded) and
portablekit_host_check builds. Not seen in a real stall: the "looping
through HLE calls", "neither" and idle verdicts.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
